	abstract = {Laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS) enables rapid, in-situ analysis of aluminum-magnesium (Al–Mg) alloys within manufacturing processes. However, accurate quantification of magnesium (Mg) across a wide concentration range is challenged by complex spectral responses, such as self-absorption and plasma dynamics. This study investigates the effects of picosecond and nanosecond lasers on LIBS analysis of Mg in Al–Mg alloys and develops a machine learning framework to improve quantification under these two scenarios. Specifically, LIBS experiments were conducted using 355 nm ps and nanosecond lasers on samples with Mg concentrations of 0.62-10.32 wt%. Picosecond lasers resulted in stronger self-absorption and nonlinear spectral signals of some Mg lines, while nanosecond lasers provided more stable, linear responses. To mitigate prediction errors arising from the low intensity and fluctuation of linear Mg lines, this work develops the Comprehensive Machine Learning and Feature Fusion for Laser-Induced Breakdown Spectroscopy (CMFFS-LIBS). This framework leverages the complementary information from high-intensity, nonlinear spectral lines to enhance prediction performance. It integrates data preprocessing (wavelet transform and Savitzky-Golay filtering), feature engineering (WBP, PCA, and XGBoost-RFE), and a Random Forest model. The proposed CMFFS-LIBS framework demonstrated good performance, achieving coefficient of determination (R<sup>2</sup>) scores of 0.939, 0.981, and 0.958 for the picosecond, nanosecond, and mixed datasets, with corresponding mean absolute errors (MAE) of 0.447, 0.166, and 0.318 wt%, respectively. This method provides a reliable strategy for online Mg quantification analysis in alloy production, achieving a data dimensionality reduction of over 94.8%, and can be extended to other elements. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
