	abstract = {A 3-year study was conducted on zirconia and titanium abutments used for single-tooth implants. Both abutments had a 100% survival rate and yielded similar outcomes. No significant differences were observed in terms of biological, technical, and esthetical outcomes. Dental implant research focuses on materials, design, and surface modification. The evolution of dental implants includes various types, design parameters, and surface trends. A review of RP methods in dentistry for prostheses fabrication found that RP techniques overcome the drawbacks of subtractive methods. RP applications in dentistry include implant surgical guides, frameworks, and complete dentures. TiZr alloys are a strong and biocompatible alternative to pure titanium. TiZr implants have high strength, corrosion resistance, and biocompatibility. Animal and clinical studies have shown that TiZr alloys achieve good Osseo integration. RP methods are used in dentistry for various applications such as implant guides, prosthesis, and molds. The application of RP techniques in dentistry presents challenges. Digital technology has revolutionized dentistry with RP techniques. Mechanical testing was conducted on thin-walled zirconia abutments for implant-supported restorations. The fatigue behavior and failure modes of straight and angled abutments were characterized. Challenges related to the treatment of dental implants as well as the methods used for mechanical testing are discussed in this article. The main focus is on characterizing the implant-prostheses complex and understanding the failure mechanisms involved. An evaluation is made of the common mechanical testing methods used in restorative dentistry, with a critical perspective. Additionally, the educational aspect for dental students regarding the morphology of permanent teeth is explored by employing 3D models. The NextEngine™ 3D Scanner HD is utilized to scan healthy permanent teeth, and virtual models are then processed using ReplicatorG software to create physical prototypes. The objective of this approach is to improve the visualization of morphological characteristics of permanent teeth. Inadequate root canal disinfection is identified as a cause for failure in endodontic restorations. While NaOCl is effective in sterilizing biofilm models, it does not have the same effect on extracted teeth. The presence of biofilm is not considered crucial for the persistence of root canal infection. In a separate study, stress patterns in cortical and cancellous bones surrounding dental implants were assessed. The static, dynamic, and fatigue behaviors of dental implants were analyzed using finite element analysis (FEA). It was observed that dynamic loading resulted in stress levels 5-10% higher compared to static loading. The inclusion of micro threads in the design of implants led to more favorable stress patterns. Lastly, an engineering analysis was conducted to evaluate the stress experienced by dental implant prosthetics. Static simulation with 3D finite element for dental implant. Four thread designs tested for stress distribution. Literature review on validation methods for FEA studies of dental implants. Limited high-level validation in dental implant FEA studies. CBCT better than IO imaging in detecting, classifying, and measuring bone defects. CBCT provides superior diagnostic accuracy and reliability over IO imaging.  © 2024 IEEE.},
