	journal = {Manufacturing Strategies and Systems Technologies, Processes, and Machine Tools},
	pages = {62 - 84},
	doi = {10.1201/9781032725086-6},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85216673544?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {A promising technique for producing intricate and substantial metal components with great efficiency and cost-effectiveness is called wire arc additive manufacturing (WAAM). In imperative to improve the quality and functionality of the made parts, with different metals and their alloys, this chapter presents a thorough investigation into the optimized progression parameters in WAAM. The crucial process parameters are then covered, including arc current, wire feed speed, torch travel speed or welding speed and arc voltage, layer height, and composition of the shielding gas, among others, and their considerable influence on the mechanical qualities, surface polish, and geometrical precision of the finished product. This work also covers the difficulties in parameter optimization, such as the parts quality and speed, and the necessity for customized solutions based on particular application needs. To demonstrate the practical application and advantages of optimized WAAM processes, case studies from an assortment of industry sectors, including aerospace, automotive, and healthcare, are presented. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Ajay Kumar, Parveen Kumar, Ashish Kumar Srivastava and Lokesh Saharan; individual chapters, the contributors.},
