	abstract = {Light-weight alloys have proven to be of paramount importance in automotive, aeronautical, and biomedical applications due to their inherent attractive properties. In literature, the machinability assessment of magnesium alloys is limited in comparison to other alloys. The machinability assessment of AZ61 magnesium alloy seems untapped as per the available literature. The current work aimed to address this gap by examining the tool wear aspect of AZ61 magnesium alloy machinability. The present study investigates the evolution of flank wear, built-up edge (BUE), and built-up layer (BUL) in different tool coatings during the dry short interval orthogonal cutting process of AZ61 magnesium alloy. The tool wear progression data presented a cyclic trend as the BUL and BUE would oscillate between minimum and maximum work piece material deposition. The tool coated in TiCN compound presented the least amount of average tool wear, while the uncoated tool showed excessive adhesion wear, abrasion wear, and chipping of the cutting edge. In level 1 machining conditions (depth of cut, 1.5 mm; feed, 0.2 mm/ rev; and cutting speed,100 m/min), tool 2 (TiN-CVD), tool 3 (uncoated), and tool 4 (nano-textured Al2O3), on average, exhibited 31.4%, 47.0%, and 64.7% more average flank wear respectively than tool 1 (TiCN-CVD). © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025.},
