	abstract = {The wire arc additive manufacturing (WAAM) process is identified as a type of directed energy deposition process, wherein metallic materials are deposited layer by layer using an arc heat source. This method enables the creation of a 3D object based on a pre-designed CAD model. This process is mostly utilized for the manufacturing of complicated design metallic parts. The manufacturing process focuses on minimal material waste, as it deviates from the traditional method of removing material from a larger block to attain the desired shape. To enhance the quality and reduce the manufacturing costs of metallic parts produced via the WAAM process, a careful selection of different process parameters such as arc current (I), arc voltage (V), travel speed, wire feed speed, and working distance is required to be optimized. This chapter reviews experimental and theoretical studies on the influences of different WAAM process parameters on bead geometry, mechanical properties, and microstructural characteristics of different metallic alloys in parts production by using the WAAM process. The quality of WAAMed parts is influenced by grain structure development during solidification. The aim of this study is to examine how WAAM process parameters affect the quality of produced WAAMed parts. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Mahendra U. Gaikwad, Ankit Sharma, Muthuramalingam Thangaraj and Sachin Salunkhe; individual chapters, the contributors.},
