	abstract = {Promoting the green transformation and upgrading of the manufacturing industry is an effective means to achieve the global common goal of carbon reduction. Additive-Subtractive Integrated Hybrid Manufacturing (ASIHM), hailed as a revolutionary frontier technology, offers significant advantages in material utilization and production efficiency. However, the comprehensive evaluation of its multi-source carbon emission characteristics and processing performance requires further research. To address this question, a theoretical energy consumption model and an actual energy consumption monitoring platform were established for the ASIHM, analyzing the carbon emission characteristics of the process and the impact of key parameters on carbon emissions. Next, the microstructure, microhardness, tensile properties, and surface roughness of the IN718 samples produced by ASIHM were tested. Subsequently, a comprehensive assessment of the carbon emission characteristics and key performance indicators of the samples was conducted. The results indicated that the difference between the theoretical carbon emission model and the actual measured energy consumption was within 6%. Under conditions of constant linear energy density, increasing scanning speed was beneficial for reducing carbon emissions. Through a comprehensive evaluation of multiple indicators, including carbon emissions and key performance metrics, the carbon emissions in the ASIHM process were reduced by 16%, while processing quality was effectively improved, achieving a balance between low carbon emissions and high performance. Finally, a comparative analysis of carbon emissions between traditional manufacturing methods and ASIHM for integral impeller processing was completed, demonstrating the effectiveness of ASIHM in material savings and carbon emission reduction. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2026.},
