	abstract = {The design and construction of the National Ignition Facility (NIF) rests on the shoulders of nearly 50 years of optical materials R&D and advanced manufacturing that evolved with the construction of prior large, Inertial Confinement Fusion (ICF) laser systems. These systems were built not just at Lawrence Livermore National Lab (LLNL), but across the US Department of Energy (DOE) and University complex as well as in the UK, Europe, Japan, Russia, and China. These international R&D efforts also addressed the key optical material needs: gain media, coatings, lenses and substrates, conversion crystals, diffraction elements, Pockels-cell crystals, deformable mirrors, etc. This wealth of past-experience and data provides a template for assessing different Inertial Fusion Energy (IFE) laser-driver designs. Such information is particularly important when developing conceptual designs, plans, timelines and costs for R&D, pilot demonstrations and full-scale manufacturing of the needed optical materials and finished optics. The historical development of Nd-doped laser glass leading to the NIF is used as an example to illustrate common challenges IFE will need to overcome to select and produce gain material at the rate and volume needed. Even though the IFE gain media may differ, the scale and complexity of the development, pilot-scale demonstrations, raw material supply-chain and full-scale production will share many common challenges with NIF and prior ICF lasers. Finally, there will be a need to build multiple IFE systems if fusion energy is to play a role in meeting future energy needs. Little attention has been given to defining this space and the total volume of optical (and other) materials that would be needed. © 2025 SPIE.},
