	abstract = {This paper for the first time conducts an in-depth comparison of the electrochemical performance between an O3-layered oxide sodium-ion battery and a mainstream lithium iron phosphate ion battery with the same prismatic external dimensions (thickness × width ×height = 71 × 173 ×207, unit: mm). The sodium-ion battery adopts a copper-based oxide (molecular formula: NaCu0.04Ni0.30Fe0.33Mn0.33O2, referred to as CNFM)/hard carbon system with an internal jellyroll structure of the laminated type, while the lithium-ion battery adopts a lithium iron phosphate (molecular formula: LiFePO4, referred to as LFP)/graphite system with an internal jellyroll structure of the wound type. The comparative test results show that the energy density of the current commercialized layered oxide sodium-ion battery has reached 82%-90% of that of lithium iron phosphate. The sodium-ion battery has significant advantages over lithium iron phosphate in terms of low-temperature discharge, fast charging and temperature rise, zero-electricity storage, SOC algorithm logic, etc. However, its shortcomings in aspects such as rate discharge temperature rise, voltage difference consistency change caused by Shakiness of the dQ/dV curve at the end of discharge, structural and electrochemical stability need to be compensated through optimizations in material involution, intelligent thermal management, smart BMS balancing management, and safety design. The comprehensive performance comparison provides beneficial support for the subsequent commercial development of sodium-ion batteries. © 2025 IEEE.},
