	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037574090?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The progressive neuro-degenerative condition known as Parkinson's disease (PD) affects millions of people worldwide and frequently starts with mild symptoms, such as speech abnormalities. Identifying this disease at an early stage is very vital, especially when vocal impairments start to manifest. Traditional diagnostic approaches fall short in identifying PD during its onset. In this work, we propose an Ensemble PCA-Deep Neural Network (PCA-DNN) framework for classifying Parkinson's disease (PD) using voice features derived from clinical data. Rather than using raw audio signals, our approach utilizes numerical voice parameters pre-extracted from available recordings. We applied standard pre-processing techniques, including feature scaling and Principal Component Analysis (PCA), to reduce complexity and noise in the dataset. A carefully designed feedforward Deep Neural Network (DNN) was trained on the reduced feature set. This model includes dropout layers and Leaky ReLU activation and demonstrated an average validation accuracy of 91% using five-fold crossvalidation with the proposed Ensemble PCA DNN model. Unlike complex systems that depend on spectrogram images or recurrent layers like LSTMs, our model is lightweight and works directly with structured data, making it suitable for fast, early PD screening in clinical settings. © 2025 IEEE.},
