	abstract = {The comprehensive integration of Closed-Circuit Television (CCTV) systems in combination with camera-enabled devices throughout public and private sectors has contributed to a substantial augmentation in the volume of video surveillance data. The process of evaluating this extensive array of video material to pinpoint abnormal activities continues to represent a considerable and intricate challenge. In reaction to this challenge, state-of-the-art deep learning frameworks, notably Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), have come forth as robust solutions for the automated detection of anomalous behaviors in video streams. By employing their organized architecture, which features convolutional layers, max-pooling layers, Rectified Linear Units (ReLU), and fully connected layers, CNNs manifest the competence to efficiently handle video frames and discern irregularities from conventional patterns. These models exhibit particular relevance in high-stakes scenarios, such as examination locations, where the deployment of real-time anomaly detection can strengthen the effectiveness of surveillance, discourage unethical behaviors, and encourage equity. Even with their notable prospects, the deployment of such systems calls for a diligent focus on ethical implications, particularly concerning user confidentiality and data protection. This survey delivers an in-depth exploration of the contemporary field of research regarding video-based anomaly detection through the application of CNNs. It underscores pivotal datasets, recent technological innovations, and emerging trends that are influencing the evolution of more resilient and intelligent surveillance frameworks. © 2025 IEEE.},
